  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM EST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nDry conditions will persist through most of the day Wednesday\ndue to high pressure over the Midwest. It will be cold tonight\nwith lows in the mid 20s but mild tomorrow with highs in the \nmid 40s. A vigorous weather system will also start moving up \ninto the region on Wednesday evening, bringing breezy east winds\nand rain into the southwest portion of the forecast area. Rain \nwill then overspread the area on Thanksgiving, with locations \nsouth of US30 seeing an inch or more of water. A few snowflakes \ncould mix in Thursday night, but confidence is low that there \nwill be any accumulations since surface temperatures should \nremain above freezing. Showers will diminish on Friday as the \nmoves into New England. \n\nDrier conditions can be expected Saturday and Sunday, although \na series of weaker shortwaves could bring light precip at times,\nwith the best chances on Sunday afternoon. DRy, zonal flow is \nexpected Monday, and possible Tuesday. Although the GFS does \nbring another wet shortwave into the region Tuesday afternoon. \nTemperatures will remain near to above normal going into the \nbeginning of December with highs in the mid to upper 40s.",
